# ♿ Accessibility Standards

## Core Principles
- Target WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance minimum
- Accessibility is not optional—it's a core requirement
- Test with keyboard only, screen readers, and automated tools

## Semantic HTML
- Use semantic elements: `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, `<header>`, `<footer>`
- Buttons for actions (`<button>`), links for navigation (`<a>`)
- Never use `<div>` or `<span>` when semantic elements exist

## Keyboard Navigation
- All interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
- Visible focus states on all focusable elements
- Logical tab order (use `tabIndex` only when necessary)
- Support standard keyboard shortcuts (Escape to close modals, Arrow keys in lists)
- No keyboard traps

## ARIA Labels
- Use ARIA attributes when semantic HTML isn't sufficient
- `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` for all interactive elements without visible text
- `aria-describedby` for additional context
- `aria-live` regions for dynamic content updates
- `role` attributes when needed (but prefer semantic HTML)

## Form Accessibility
- Always pair `<label>` with form inputs using `htmlFor`
- Visible labels (not just placeholders)
- Clear error messages associated with inputs via `aria-describedby`
- Mark required fields with `aria-required="true"`
- Group related inputs with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`

## Color and Contrast
- Minimum 4.5:1 contrast ratio for normal text
- Minimum 3:1 for large text (18px+ or 14px+ bold)
- Don't rely on color alone to convey information
- Support both light and dark modes with proper contrast

## Images and Media
- All images must have meaningful `alt` text
- Decorative images: `alt=""` (empty, not missing)
- Complex images: detailed description in `aria-describedby`
- Videos: captions and transcripts
- Audio: transcripts

## Dynamic Content
- Announce dynamic changes with `aria-live` regions
- Manage focus when content changes (modals, route changes)
- Loading states must be announced to screen readers
- Skip links for repetitive navigation

## Testing Requirements
- Run axe DevTools or similar on every new component
- Test keyboard-only navigation
- Test with screen reader (VoiceOver on Mac, NVDA on Windows)
- Include accessibility checks in CI/CD

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

### ❌ Div soup for interactive elements
```jsx
<div onClick={handleClick}>Click me</div>
```

### ✅ Use button
```jsx
<button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>
```

---

### ❌ Missing labels
```jsx
<input type="text" placeholder="Email" />
```

### ✅ Visible label
```jsx
<label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
<input id="email" type="text" />
```

---

### ❌ Color-only indicators
```css
.error { color: red; }
```

### ✅ Icons and text
```jsx
<span className="text-red-600">
  <XIcon className="inline" aria-hidden="true" />
  Error: Invalid email
</span>
```

---

### ❌ Non-descriptive links
```jsx
<a href="/learn-more">Click here</a>
```

### ✅ Descriptive links
```jsx
<a href="/learn-more">Learn more about our pricing</a>
```

## Quick Checklist

Before shipping any UI component:

- [ ] Can be navigated with keyboard only
- [ ] Has visible focus indicators
- [ ] Uses semantic HTML elements
- [ ] Has proper ARIA labels where needed
- [ ] Images have alt text
- [ ] Forms have associated labels
- [ ] Color contrast meets WCAG AA
- [ ] Tested with screen reader
- [ ] Tested with axe DevTools (no violations)
- [ ] Dynamic content is announced
- [ ] Modals trap and restore focus properly
